\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 359 PM MST, local law enforcement reported heavy rain in the\nwarned area. Flooding is already occurring. Between 1.5 and 3\ninches of rain have fallen.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nDurango, Blanding, Mexican Hat, Cortez, Pagosa Springs, Dolores,\nDove Creek, Ignacio, Aneth, Montezuma Creek, White Mesa, Bluff,\nTselakai Dezza, Halchita, Goulding, Hovenweep National Monument,\nTowaoc, Arriola, Yellow Jacket and Lewis.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T16:15:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; San Miguel, CO; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.616c4025c42723c2fe6d8b10282486a4525dd113.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.616c4025c42723c2fe6d8b10282486a4525dd113.001.1"},{"event":"Winter Storm Warning","headline":"Winter Storm Warning issued November 1 at 7:12AM MST until November 2 at 12:00PM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Heavy snow expected above 10, 000 feet. Total snow\naccumulations between 12 and 18 inches, with locally higher amounts\npossible. Winds gusting as high as 45 mph.\n\n* WHERE...Northwest San Juan Mountains and Southwest San Juan\nMountains.\n\n* WHEN...Until noon MST Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Travel could be very difficult to impossible. The\nhazardous conditions will impact the Monday morning commute.","ends":"2026-11-02T12:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.345a6edf1d4d5f8da388904eff3994d95d65cedd.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.345a6edf1d4d5f8da388904eff3994d95d65cedd.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 31 at 10:37PM MDT until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and southeast\nUtah, including the following areas, Canyonlands/Natural Bridges,\nLa Sal and Abajo Mountains and Southeast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...From 5 AM MST Sunday through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recently burned areas, and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- 1 to 3 inches of rain is expected across portions of southeast\nUtah and southwest Colorado from Sunday morning through Monday\nmorning over soils that are still saturated from recent rainfall\nand flooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Southwest San Juan Mountains; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.28dceea107844d87e801d5ba537294d1bcbe9f7e.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.28dceea107844d87e801d5ba537294d1bcbe9f7e.001.1"},{"event":"Avalanche Advisory","headline":"Avalanche Advisory issued October 31 at 7:16PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"SABCO\n\nTHE FOLLOWING MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at\nwww.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\nBeyer & Pritchett","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-11-01T23:17:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 012317 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 417 PM MST Sun Nov 1 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - The Flood Watch continues for southeast Utah and southwest Colorado as an atmospheric river brings rain, rainshowers, and snowfall to the area. - Several rivers across southwest Colorado are forecast to reach minor flooding stage. - The San Juan River at Pa","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-11-01T18:59:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... This is an automatically generated forecast with no discussion. The normal Fire Weather Forecast will resume in its entirety in the spring. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS CAN BE EXPECTED NEAR ANY SHOWERS OR THUNDERSTORMS... ...THE SMOKE DATA IS CALCULATED FROM AN AVERAGE AT 6000 FEET MSL... COZ200-012300- Little Snake Forecast Area- 1159 AM MST Sun Nov 1 2026","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":{"station_id":"rgw01","station_name":"Ridgway","distance_km":27.8,"soil_temp_5cm_f":47.57,"soil_temp_15cm_f":47.52,"soil_moisture_5cm":null,"soil_moisture_15cm":null,"precip_in":null,"eto_in":null,"vapor_pressure":null,"solar_radiation":0.08,"wind_speed_mph":4.2,"air_temp_f":4